---
/**
 * /vina-del-mar-safety/ — EN city editorial page (non-FAQ)
 *
 * Loads Viña del Mar (CUT 5109) via loadCommune. DataCallouts for rate vs national + trend.
 * WebPage JSON-LD. esPath=/es/seguridad-vina-del-mar/ (EDIT-02 verbatim).
 *
 * CRITICAL: uses loadNationalAverage() (MEAN), latestCompleteYearRate() — never SUM.
 */
import EditorialLayout from '../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro';
import DataCallout from '../components/DataCallout.astro';
import { loadCommune, loadNationalAverage, latestCompleteYearRate } from '../lib/data.ts';

const vina = loadCommune('5109');
const year = vina.latestCompleteYear;
const rate = latestCompleteYearRate(vina);
const nationalAvg = loadNationalAverage();
const vsNationalPct = nationalAvg > 0
  ? Math.round(((rate - nationalAvg) / nationalAvg) * 100)
  : 0;
const trendLabel =
  vina.trend === 'up' ? 'rising' :
  vina.trend === 'down' ? 'falling' :
  'stable';

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  "name": "Viña del Mar Safety — CEAD Crime Data",
  "description": "Reported crime statistics for Viña del Mar commune from CEAD official data.",
  "about": {
    "@type": "Dataset",
    "name": "CEAD Crime Statistics — Viña del Mar",
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "GovernmentOrganization",
      "name": "Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito (CEAD)",
    },
  },
};
---

<EditorialLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Viña del Mar Safety — Crime Statistics and Data Overview — Chile Safety Map"
  description="Official CEAD crime statistics for Viña del Mar commune. Reported rates, trend direction, national comparison, and practical context for visitors and residents."
  enPath="/vina-del-mar-safety/"
  esPath="/es/seguridad-vina-del-mar/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section>
    <h1>Viña del Mar Safety: CEAD Crime Data for Chile's Garden City</h1>
    <p>
      Viña del Mar is Chile's principal seaside resort city, separated from Valparaíso by a
      short stretch of coastline but forming a distinct commune with its own character. Its
      beach areas, casino, and residential zones attract large numbers of visitors during
      summer. This page reports what CEAD official data shows for the commune of Viña del Mar
      for {year}.
    </p>

    <DataCallout
      value={`${Math.round(rate).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100k`}
      label={`Viña del Mar commune reported crime rate — CEAD ${year}`}
      source="Source: CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito)"
      locale="en"
    />

    <DataCallout
      value={`${Math.round(nationalAvg).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100k`}
      label={`National average reported crime rate — CEAD ${year}`}
      source="Source: CEAD — unweighted mean of non-low-population communes"
      locale="en"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How Viña del Mar Compares with the National Average</h2>
    <p>
      According to CEAD data for {year}, Viña del Mar's reported crime rate of
      {Math.round(rate).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100,000 inhabitants is approximately
      {Math.abs(vsNationalPct)} % {vsNationalPct >= 0 ? 'above' : 'below'} the national
      mean of {Math.round(nationalAvg).toLocaleString('en-US')} per 100,000. The current
      trend is {trendLabel} according to the CEAD series for recent years.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full year-by-year series for Viña del Mar — including breakdown by crime family —
      is available on the <a href="/commune/vina-del-mar/">Viña del Mar commune detail page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Seasonal Variation and Beach Areas</h2>
    <p>
      Viña del Mar's summer season (December through March) brings a substantial increase in
      visitors to its beach areas and casino zone. As with any popular tourist destination,
      opportunistic property crime — bag theft, phone snatching, theft from unattended
      belongings on the beach — is reported more frequently during peak periods.
    </p>
    <p>
      CEAD data is published as annual totals and does not break out seasonal variation
      within a year. The annual rate therefore reflects a blend of peak-season and off-season
      incident patterns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Zones Within the Commune</h2>
    <p>
      Viña del Mar spans from the beach front along Avenida España north through the casino
      and shopping district of Viña Centro, then into the residential eastern and southern
      zones of Villa Alemana and the inland hills. Incident patterns within the commune are
      not resolved to neighbourhood level by CEAD data, which reports at commune granularity.
    </p>
    <p>
      The beach front and casino area represent the highest foot traffic and are the primary
      zones for property crime reports. Residential inland zones generally experience lower
      incident densities.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Crime Type Composition</h2>
    <p>
      Property crime (robbery and theft) represents the largest share of reported incidents
      in Viña del Mar according to CEAD data, consistent with most urban communes and
      particularly pronounced in coastal tourist zones. The crime-family breakdown for each
      year is visible on the <a href="/commune/vina-del-mar/">commune detail page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Viña del Mar and Nearby Communes</h2>
    <p>
      Viña del Mar is part of the greater Valparaíso conurbation. The neighbouring commune
      of Valparaíso (the port city) has a distinct rate profile — see
      <a href="/valparaiso-safety/">Valparaíso safety data</a> for comparison. Other communes
      in the Valparaíso Province, including Quilpué and Villa Alemana further inland, show
      yet different rate profiles. The <a href="/region/valparaiso/">Valparaíso region
      ranking</a> lets you compare all communes in the region.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Practical Context for Visitors and Residents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Beachfront areas during summer attract high foot traffic and the property crime
        patterns associated with it. Secure valuables; do not leave bags or electronic
        devices unattended on the beach.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Casino Municipal area and Avenida Valparaíso shopping street are busy zones
        where standard urban caution applies, particularly after dark.
      </li>
      <li>
        Residential eastern zones are primarily suburban in character and show lower
        incident densities, consistent with CEAD data for comparable residential communes.
      </li>
      <li>
        For long-term residents evaluating specific neighbourhoods, local knowledge and
        visits at different times supplement what commune-level CEAD data can show.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Data Source and Methodology</h2>
    <p>
      All statistics on this page come from CEAD (Centro de Estudios y Análisis del Delito),
      the official Chilean government crime statistics body, covering police-reported incidents
      for {year}. Rates are computed per 100,000 registered inhabitants. The national average
      is the unweighted mean across non-low-population communes. Full methodology is documented
      on the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialLayout>

<style>
  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--2xl);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-top: var(--md);
  }

  ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: var(--lg);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
